um_number_of_scale_intervals: {\"value\": {\"min\": null, \"max\": 500}, \"unit_symbol\": \"divisions\", \"unit_id\": null}\n- temperature_range: {\"value\": {\"min\": 5, \"max\": 35}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null}\n- power_supply_voltage: {\"value\": 3.7, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\", \"unit_id\": null}\n- power_supply_type: {\"value\": \"DC\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n- application: {\"value\": \"To be used only for weighing patients\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n- software_identification: {\"value\": \"P1.xx (xx = 00 ~ 99)\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null}\n\nMaybe add power_supply: \"battery\". The text \"3,7 V DC by the battery\" — power source is battery. I'll include \"power_supply_source\": {\"value\": \"battery\"}. Hmm, keep it simple; the rule 3 example splits voltage/type/frequency. Battery is extra info; including it is fine but could be considered noise. I'll include it as it's informative: \"power_source\": {\"value\": \"battery\"}.\n\nFinal JSON. Return only JSON.",
